INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$70feb725$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$029548-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">During a month, Morgan ran <em class="inline_variable ">r</em>&nbsp;miles at 5&nbsp;miles per hour and biked <em class="inline_variable ">b</em>&nbsp;miles at 10&nbsp;miles per hour. She ran and biked a total of 200&nbsp;miles that month, and she biked for twice as many hours as she ran. What is the total number of miles that Morgan biked during the month?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">80</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">100</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">120</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">160</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. The number of hours Morgan spent running or biking can be calculated by dividing the distance she traveled during that activity by her speed, in miles per hour, for that activity. So the number of hours she ran can be represented by the expression <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_000_2e31900c.png" alt="the fraction r over 5"></span>, and the number of hours she biked can be represented by the expression <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_001_bdd21fcc.png" alt="the fraction b over 10"></span>. It&rsquo;s given that she biked for twice as many hours as she ran, so this can be represented by the equation <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_002_fcbb48d0.png" alt="the fraction b over 10, end fraction,  equals, 2 times the fraction r over 5"></span>, which can be rewritten as <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_003_44f8bd2e.png" alt="b equals 4 r"></span>. It&rsquo;s also given that she ran <span class="italic">r</span> miles and biked <span class="italic">b</span> miles, and that she ran and biked a total of 200 miles. This can be represented by the equation <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_004_e8214ca2.png" alt="r plus b, equals 200 "></span>. Substituting <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_005_fcc5617e.png" alt="4 r"></span> for <span class="italic">b</span> in this equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_006_0e646158.png" alt="r plus 4 r, equals 200 "></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_007_9cdca1bb.png" alt="5 r equals 200"></span>. Solving for <span class="italic">r</span>&nbsp; yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_008_2392a5e0.png" alt="r equals 40"></span>. Determining the number of miles she biked, <span class="italic">b</span>, can be found by substituting 40 for <span class="italic">r</span> in <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_009_e8214ca2.png" alt="r plus b, equals 200 "></span>, which yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_010_834723fe.png" alt="40 plus b, equals 200"></span>. Solving for <span class="italic">b</span> yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_011_a5c22335.png" alt="b equals 160"></span>.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect because they don&rsquo;t satisfy that Morgan biked for twice as many hours as she ran. In choice A, if she biked 80 miles, then she ran 120 miles, which means she biked for 8 hours and ran for 24 hours. In choice B, if she biked 100 miles, then she ran 100 miles, which means she biked for 10 hours and ran for 20 hours. In choice C, if she biked 120 miles, then she ran for 80 miles, which means she biked for 12 hours and ran for 16 hours.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 12, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"70feb725","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"029548-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.D.","skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">During a month, Morgan ran <em class=\"inline_variable \">r</em>&nbsp;miles at 5&nbsp;miles per hour and biked <em class=\"inline_variable \">b</em>&nbsp;miles at 10&nbsp;miles per hour. She ran and biked a total of 200&nbsp;miles that month, and she biked for twice as many hours as she ran. What is the total number of miles that Morgan biked during the month?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">80</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">100</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">120</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">160</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. The number of hours Morgan spent running or biking can be calculated by dividing the distance she traveled during that activity by her speed, in miles per hour, for that activity. So the number of hours she ran can be represented by the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_000_2e31900c.png\" alt=\"the fraction r over 5\"></span>, and the number of hours she biked can be represented by the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_001_bdd21fcc.png\" alt=\"the fraction b over 10\"></span>. It&rsquo;s given that she biked for twice as many hours as she ran, so this can be represented by the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_002_fcbb48d0.png\" alt=\"the fraction b over 10, end fraction,  equals, 2 times the fraction r over 5\"></span>, which can be rewritten as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_003_44f8bd2e.png\" alt=\"b equals 4 r\"></span>. It&rsquo;s also given that she ran <span class=\"italic\">r</span> miles and biked <span class=\"italic\">b</span> miles, and that she ran and biked a total of 200 miles. This can be represented by the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_004_e8214ca2.png\" alt=\"r plus b, equals 200 \"></span>. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_005_fcc5617e.png\" alt=\"4 r\"></span> for <span class=\"italic\">b</span> in this equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_006_0e646158.png\" alt=\"r plus 4 r, equals 200 \"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_007_9cdca1bb.png\" alt=\"5 r equals 200\"></span>. Solving for <span class=\"italic\">r</span>&nbsp; yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_008_2392a5e0.png\" alt=\"r equals 40\"></span>. Determining the number of miles she biked, <span class=\"italic\">b</span>, can be found by substituting 40 for <span class=\"italic\">r</span> in <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_009_e8214ca2.png\" alt=\"r plus b, equals 200 \"></span>, which yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_010_834723fe.png\" alt=\"40 plus b, equals 200\"></span>. Solving for <span class=\"italic\">b</span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/70feb725/img_011_a5c22335.png\" alt=\"b equals 160\"></span>.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect because they don&rsquo;t satisfy that Morgan biked for twice as many hours as she ran. In choice A, if she biked 80 miles, then she ran 120 miles, which means she biked for 8 hours and ran for 24 hours. In choice B, if she biked 100 miles, then she ran 100 miles, which means she biked for 10 hours and ran for 20 hours. In choice C, if she biked 120 miles, then she ran for 80 miles, which means she biked for 12 hours and ran for 16 hours.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":12,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959626,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"70feb725","skill_cd":"H.D.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","createDate":1691007959626,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"029548-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"527007bd-1add-4ad6-8945-cf22556c7289","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"029548-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">During a month, Morgan ran <em class=\"inline_variable \">r</em>&nbsp;miles at 5&nbsp;miles per hour and biked <em class=\"inline_variable \">b</em>&nbsp;miles at 10&nbsp;miles per hour. She ran and biked a total of 200&nbsp;miles that month, and she biked for twice as many hours as she ran. What is the total number of miles that Morgan biked during the month?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">80</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">100</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">120</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">160</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"d","rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. The number of hours Morgan spent running or biking can be calculated by dividing the distance she traveled during that activity by her speed, in miles per hour, for that activity. So the number of hours she ran can be represented by the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction r over 5\"></span>, and the number of hours she biked can be represented by the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction b over 10\"></span>. It&rsquo;s given that she biked for twice as many hours as she ran, so this can be represented by the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction b over 10, end fraction,  equals, 2 times the fraction r over 5\"></span>, which can be rewritten as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b equals 4 r\"></span>. It&rsquo;s also given that she ran <span class=\"italic\">r</span> miles and biked <span class=\"italic\">b</span> miles, and that she ran and biked a total of 200 miles. This can be represented by the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r plus b, equals 200 \"></span>. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"4 r\"></span> for <span class=\"italic\">b</span> in this equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r plus 4 r, equals 200 \"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"5 r equals 200\"></span>. Solving for <span class=\"italic\">r</span>&nbsp; yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r equals 40\"></span>. Determining the number of miles she biked, <span class=\"italic\">b</span>, can be found by substituting 40 for <span class=\"italic\">r</span> in <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r plus b, equals 200 \"></span>, which yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"40 plus b, equals 200\"></span>. Solving for <span class=\"italic\">b</span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"b equals 160\"></span>.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect because they don&rsquo;t satisfy that Morgan biked for twice as many hours as she ran. In choice A, if she biked 80 miles, then she ran 120 miles, which means she biked for 8 hours and ran for 24 hours. In choice B, if she biked 100 miles, then she ran 100 miles, which means she biked for 10 hours and ran for 20 hours. In choice C, if she biked 120 miles, then she ran for 80 miles, which means she biked for 12 hours and ran for 16 hours.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959626,"updateDate":1691007959626}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959626, 1691007959626),
    ($SATQ$71189542$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$09372-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A group of 202&nbsp;people went on an overnight camping trip, taking 60&nbsp;tents with them. Some of the tents held 2&nbsp;people each, and the rest held 4&nbsp;people each. Assuming all the tents were filled to capacity and every person got to sleep in a tent, exactly how many of the tents were 2-person tents?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p>30</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>20</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>19</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>18</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is correct. Let <span class="italic">x</span> represent the number of 2-person tents and let <span class="italic">y</span> represent the number of 4-person tents. It is given that the total number of tents was 60 and the total number of people in the group was 202. This situation can be expressed as a system of two equations, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_000_049f0734.png" alt="x plus y, equals 60"></span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_001_1badbef3.png" alt="2 x plus 4 y, equals 202"></span>. The first equation can be rewritten as <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_002_8777bb2f.png" alt="y equals, negative x plus 60"></span>. Substituting <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_003_41b99950.png" alt="negative x plus 60"></span> for <span class="italic">y</span> in the equation <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_004_1badbef3.png" alt="2 x plus 4 y, equals 202"></span> yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_005_4cdc1431.png" alt="2 x plus, 4 times, open parenthesis, negative x plus 60, close parenthesis, equals 202"></span>. Distributing and combining like terms gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_006_2c3fcc28.png" alt="negative 2 x plus 240, equals 202"></span>. Subtracting 240 from both sides of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_007_2c3fcc28.png" alt="negative 2 x plus 240, equals 202"></span> and then dividing both sides by <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_008_ebe7b417.png" alt="negative 2"></span> gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_009_6ae5fc8d.png" alt="x equals 19"></span>. Therefore, the number of 2-person tents is 19.<p>Alternate approach: If each of the 60 tents held 4 people, the total number of people that could be accommodated in tents would be 240. However, the actual number of people who slept in tents was 202. The difference of 38 accounts for the 2-person tents. Since each of these tents holds 2 people fewer than a 4-person tent, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_010_e99516de.png" alt="thirty eight halves, equals 19"></span> gives the number of 2-person tents.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice may result from assuming exactly half of the tents hold 2 people. If that were true, then the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_011_5c1f6783.png" alt="2 times 30, plus, 4 times 30, equals 180"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 180. Choice B is incorrect. If 20 tents were 2-person tents, then the remaining 40 tents would be 4-person tents. Since all the tents were filled to capacity, the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_012_a69d5c6e.png" alt="2 times 20, plus, 4 times 40, equals, 40 plus 160, which equals 200"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 200. Choice D is incorrect. If 18 tents were 2-person tents, then the remaining 42 tents would be 4-person tents. Since all the tents were filled to capacity, the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_013_ed676a25.png" alt="2 times 18, plus, 4 times 42, equals, 36 plus 168, which equals 204"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 204. <!--EndFragment--></p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 14, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"71189542","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"09372-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.D.","skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A group of 202&nbsp;people went on an overnight camping trip, taking 60&nbsp;tents with them. Some of the tents held 2&nbsp;people each, and the rest held 4&nbsp;people each. Assuming all the tents were filled to capacity and every person got to sleep in a tent, exactly how many of the tents were 2-person tents?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p>30</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>20</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>19</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>18</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">x</span> represent the number of 2-person tents and let <span class=\"italic\">y</span> represent the number of 4-person tents. It is given that the total number of tents was 60 and the total number of people in the group was 202. This situation can be expressed as a system of two equations, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_000_049f0734.png\" alt=\"x plus y, equals 60\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_001_1badbef3.png\" alt=\"2 x plus 4 y, equals 202\"></span>. The first equation can be rewritten as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_002_8777bb2f.png\" alt=\"y equals, negative x plus 60\"></span>. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_003_41b99950.png\" alt=\"negative x plus 60\"></span> for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> in the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_004_1badbef3.png\" alt=\"2 x plus 4 y, equals 202\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_005_4cdc1431.png\" alt=\"2 x plus, 4 times, open parenthesis, negative x plus 60, close parenthesis, equals 202\"></span>. Distributing and combining like terms gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_006_2c3fcc28.png\" alt=\"negative 2 x plus 240, equals 202\"></span>. Subtracting 240 from both sides of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_007_2c3fcc28.png\" alt=\"negative 2 x plus 240, equals 202\"></span> and then dividing both sides by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_008_ebe7b417.png\" alt=\"negative 2\"></span> gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_009_6ae5fc8d.png\" alt=\"x equals 19\"></span>. Therefore, the number of 2-person tents is 19.<p>Alternate approach: If each of the 60 tents held 4 people, the total number of people that could be accommodated in tents would be 240. However, the actual number of people who slept in tents was 202. The difference of 38 accounts for the 2-person tents. Since each of these tents holds 2 people fewer than a 4-person tent, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_010_e99516de.png\" alt=\"thirty eight halves, equals 19\"></span> gives the number of 2-person tents.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice may result from assuming exactly half of the tents hold 2 people. If that were true, then the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_011_5c1f6783.png\" alt=\"2 times 30, plus, 4 times 30, equals 180\"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 180. Choice B is incorrect. If 20 tents were 2-person tents, then the remaining 40 tents would be 4-person tents. Since all the tents were filled to capacity, the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_012_a69d5c6e.png\" alt=\"2 times 20, plus, 4 times 40, equals, 40 plus 160, which equals 200\"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 200. Choice D is incorrect. If 18 tents were 2-person tents, then the remaining 42 tents would be 4-person tents. Since all the tents were filled to capacity, the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/71189542/img_013_ed676a25.png\" alt=\"2 times 18, plus, 4 times 42, equals, 36 plus 168, which equals 204\"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 204. <!--EndFragment--></p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":14,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959637,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"71189542","skill_cd":"H.D.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","createDate":1691007959637,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"09372-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"2d6cadce-4a6b-490d-b122-9f54b59140ea","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"09372-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A group of 202&nbsp;people went on an overnight camping trip, taking 60&nbsp;tents with them. Some of the tents held 2&nbsp;people each, and the rest held 4&nbsp;people each. Assuming all the tents were filled to capacity and every person got to sleep in a tent, exactly how many of the tents were 2-person tents?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p>30</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p>20</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p>19</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p>18</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"c","rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">x</span> represent the number of 2-person tents and let <span class=\"italic\">y</span> represent the number of 4-person tents. It is given that the total number of tents was 60 and the total number of people in the group was 202. This situation can be expressed as a system of two equations, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x plus y, equals 60\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 x plus 4 y, equals 202\"></span>. The first equation can be rewritten as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"y equals, negative x plus 60\"></span>. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative x plus 60\"></span> for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> in the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 x plus 4 y, equals 202\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 x plus, 4 times, open parenthesis, negative x plus 60, close parenthesis, equals 202\"></span>. Distributing and combining like terms gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 2 x plus 240, equals 202\"></span>. Subtracting 240 from both sides of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 2 x plus 240, equals 202\"></span> and then dividing both sides by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 2\"></span> gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals 19\"></span>. Therefore, the number of 2-person tents is 19.<p>Alternate approach: If each of the 60 tents held 4 people, the total number of people that could be accommodated in tents would be 240. However, the actual number of people who slept in tents was 202. The difference of 38 accounts for the 2-person tents. Since each of these tents holds 2 people fewer than a 4-person tent, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"thirty eight halves, equals 19\"></span> gives the number of 2-person tents.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice may result from assuming exactly half of the tents hold 2 people. If that were true, then the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times 30, plus, 4 times 30, equals 180\"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 180. Choice B is incorrect. If 20 tents were 2-person tents, then the remaining 40 tents would be 4-person tents. Since all the tents were filled to capacity, the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times 20, plus, 4 times 40, equals, 40 plus 160, which equals 200\"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 200. Choice D is incorrect. If 18 tents were 2-person tents, then the remaining 42 tents would be 4-person tents. Since all the tents were filled to capacity, the total number of people who slept in tents would be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times 18, plus, 4 times 42, equals, 36 plus 168, which equals 204\"></span>; however, the total number of people who slept in tents was 202, not 204. <!--EndFragment--></p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959637,"updateDate":1691007959637}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959637, 1691007959637),
