    ($SATQ$74c98c82$SATQ$, $SATQ$ff06a0a2-65a5-41cc-a2c6-f909deb61b07$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$98e69211-1b3a-486c-baf6-090b428cd7e7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear inequalities in one or two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">An event planner is planning a party. It costs the event planner a onetime fee of <math alttext="dollar sign 35"><mo>$</mo><mn>35</mn>
</math> to rent the venue and <math alttext="dollar sign 10.25"><mo>$</mo><mn>10.25</mn>
</math> per attendee. The event planner has a budget of <math alttext="dollar sign 200"><mo>$</mo><mn>200</mn>
</math>. What is the greatest number of attendees possible without exceeding the budget?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["16"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["16"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <math alttext="16"><mn>16</mn>
</math>. The total cost of the party is found by adding the onetime fee of the venue to the cost per attendee times the number of attendees. Let <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> be the number of attendees. The expression <math alttext="35 plus 10.25 x"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi></math> thus represents the total cost of the party. It's given that the budget is <math alttext="dollar sign 200"><mo>$</mo><mn>200</mn></math>, so this situation can be represented by the inequality <math alttext="35 plus 10.25 x less than or equals 200"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>200</mn></math>. The greatest number of attendees can be found by solving this inequality for <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math>. Subtracting <math alttext="35"><mn>35</mn>
</math> from both sides of this inequality gives <math alttext="10.25 x less than or equals 165"><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>165</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this inequality by <math alttext="10.25"><mn>10.25</mn>
</math> results in approximately <math alttext="x less than or equals 16.098"><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16.098</mn></math>. Since the question is stated in terms of attendees, rounding <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> down to the nearest whole number, <math alttext="16"><mn>16</mn>
</math>, gives the greatest number of attendees possible.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"74c98c82","external_id":"ff06a0a2-65a5-41cc-a2c6-f909deb61b07","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"98e69211-1b3a-486c-baf6-090b428cd7e7","uId":"c8efc8eb-f976-4ffe-b4a7-a2147b2f7dda","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.E.","skill_desc":"Linear inequalities in one or two variables","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">An event planner is planning a party. It costs the event planner a onetime fee of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 35\"><mo>$</mo><mn>35</mn>\n</math> to rent the venue and <math alttext=\"dollar sign 10.25\"><mo>$</mo><mn>10.25</mn>\n</math> per attendee. The event planner has a budget of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 200\"><mo>$</mo><mn>200</mn>\n</math>. What is the greatest number of attendees possible without exceeding the budget?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["16"],"correct_answer":["16"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is <math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math>. The total cost of the party is found by adding the onetime fee of the venue to the cost per attendee times the number of attendees. Let <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> be the number of attendees. The expression <math alttext=\"35 plus 10.25 x\"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi></math> thus represents the total cost of the party. It's given that the budget is <math alttext=\"dollar sign 200\"><mo>$</mo><mn>200</mn></math>, so this situation can be represented by the inequality <math alttext=\"35 plus 10.25 x less than or equals 200\"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>200</mn></math>. The greatest number of attendees can be found by solving this inequality for <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>. Subtracting <math alttext=