<p style="text-align: left;">One of the two equations in a system of linear equations is given. The system has infinitely many solutions. Which equation could be the second equation in this system?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"af5916c7-f809-45e2-8da7-172c381d084e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"y equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x right parenthesis plus 3\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"42fefd99-87b6-487e-bc8e-1b62202eaca8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"y equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"4fb8daa4-4793-4bd1-b66a-819f0ee13ae1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x right parenthesis plus 3\"><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"b573c386-beba-442f-89b1-a1de856c0e68","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b573c386-beba-442f-89b1-a1de856c0e68"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the system has infinitely many solutions. A system of two linear equations has infinitely many solutions when the two linear equations are equivalent. When one equation is a multiple of another equation, the two equations are equivalent. Multiplying each side of the given equation by <math alttext="2"><mn>2</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis"><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi>y</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Thus, <math alttext="2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis"><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi>y</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> is equivalent to the given equation and could be the second equation in the system.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. The system consisting of this equation and the given equation has one solution rather than infinitely many solutions.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. The system consisting of this equation and the given equation has one solution rather than infinitely many solutions.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. The system consisting of this equation and the given equation has no solutions rather than infinitely many solutions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"79784c23","external_id":"73c4251e-9b52-43e0-b64c-eafae656c149","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"9bfe2636-c62f-4cbd-aed8-8f8604201c35","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.D.","skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"y equals 6 x plus 3\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>6</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">One of the two equations in a system of linear equations is given. The system has infinitely many solutions. Which equation could be the second equation in this system?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"af5916c7-f809-45e2-8da7-172c381d084e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"y equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x right parenthesis plus 3\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"42fefd99-87b6-487e-bc8e-1b62202eaca8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"y equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"4fb8daa4-4793-4bd1-b66a-819f0ee13ae1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x right parenthesis plus 3\"><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"b573c386-beba-442f-89b1-a1de856c0e68","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p>"}],"keys":["b573c386-beba-442f-89b1-a1de856c0e68"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the system has infinitely many solutions. A system of two linear equations has infinitely many solutions when the two linear equations are equivalent. When one equation is a multiple of another equation, the two equations are equivalent. Multiplying each side of the given equation by <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi>y</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Thus, <math alttext=\"2 left parenthesis y right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 6 x plus 3 right parenthesis\"><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi>y</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> is equivalent to the given equation and could be the second equation in the system.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. The system consisting of this equation and the given equation has one solution rather than infinitely many solutions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. The system consisting of this equation and the given equation has one solution rather than infinitely many solutions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. The system consisting of this equation and the given equation has no solutions rather than infinitely many solutions.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,